<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · c. 1934 · Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</p><h1 class="sg-title">My Homeland</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ar" dir="rtl">موطني</bdi> / <bdi lang="ar-Latn">Mawṭinī</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song of love for the homeland, written in 1934 by a Palestinian poet. It is now the national anthem of Iraq.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Ibrahim Tuqan</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Mohammed Flayfel</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Palestinians and the wider Arab world; Iraq since 2004</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>c. 1934</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Ibrahim Tuqan (c. 1934; he died in 1941))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Mawṭinī&quot; (&quot;My Homeland&quot;) is an Arabic patriotic poem by the Palestinian poet Ibrahim Tuqan, written around 1934 and set to music by the Lebanese composer Mohammed Flayfel, who also composed &quot;Humat ad-Diyar&quot;, the national anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. It became a popular patriotic song across the Arab world.</p><p>For much of the 20th century it served as an unofficial anthem of the Palestinians and of other peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>. In 1996 &quot;Fidā'ī&quot; officially took its place as the Palestinian national anthem, but many Palestinians still identify strongly with &quot;Mawṭinī&quot; and regard it as a second, unofficial national anthem.</p><p>In 2004 it became the national anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, on the order of Paul Bremer, the head of the Coalition Provisional Authority. Iraq had used an earlier, unrelated anthem of the same name: an instrumental &quot;Mawṭinī&quot; by Lewis Zanbaka, played in the late 1950s and early 1960s after Iraq became a republic. After the Ba'athist government was overthrown in 2003, that older piece was brought back for a short time in place of &quot;Arḍulfurātayni&quot;, the anthem in use since 1981 and associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein">Saddam Hussein</a>'s government, until Tuqan's song replaced it in 2004.</p><p>The song is widely seen as a reflection of the shared experiences of Arab peoples, and as a symbol of resistance and of the longing for liberation under occupation and oppression. Many Arab artists have recorded it anew; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dido">Elissa</a>, Christina Sawaya and Faia Younan sang it in a video clip depicting the Syrian civil war.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of deep love for the homeland and of commitment to its freedom. Its feelings move between sorrow, anger and hope, toward a vision of a free and united nation.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Arabic</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Arabic</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">مَوْطِنِي مَوْطِنِي</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Mawtinī mawtinī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My homeland, my homeland,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">اَلْجَلَالُ وَالْجَمَالُ وَالسَّنَاءُ وَالْبَهَاءُ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Al-jalālu wa-al-jamālu wa-al-sanāʾu wa-al-bahāʾu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>majesty and beauty, radiance and splendour</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">فِي رُبَاكْ فِي رُبَاكْ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Fī rubāk fī rubāk</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>are in your hills, in your hills;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">وَالْحَيَاةُ وَالنَّجَاةُ وَالْهَنَاءُ وَالرَّجَاءُ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Wa-al-hayātu wa-al-najātu wa-al-hanāʾu wa-al-rajāʾu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>life and deliverance, delight and hope</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">فِي هَوَاكْ فِي هَوَاكْ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Fī hawāk fī hawāk</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>are in your air, in your air.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">هَلْ أَرَاكْ هَلْ أَرَاكْ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Hal arāk hal arāk</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Shall I see you, shall I see you</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">سَالِمًا مُنَعَّمًا وَغَانِمًا مُكَرَّمًا</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Sāliman munaʿʿaman wa-ghāniman mukarraman</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>safe, living in ease, victorious and honoured?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">هَلْ أَرَاكْ فِي عُلَاكْ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Hal arāk fī ʿulāk</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Shall I see you in your height</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">تَبْلُغُ السِّمَاكْ تَبْلُغُ السِّمَاكْ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Tablughu al-simāk tablughu al-simāk</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>reaching al-Simak, reaching al-Simak?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">مَوْطِنِي مَوْطِنِي</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Mawtinī mawtinī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My homeland, my homeland.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">مَوْطِنِي مَوْطِنِي</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Mawtinī mawtinī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My homeland, my homeland,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">اَلشَّبَابُ لَنْ يَكِلَّ هَمُّهُ أَنْ يَسْتَقِلَّ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Al-shabābu lan yakilla hammuhu an yastaqilla</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the youth will not tire; their care is that you be independent</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">أَوْ يَبِيدْ أَوْ يَبِيدْ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Aw yabīd aw yabīd</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>or they perish, or they perish.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">نَسْتَقِي مِنَ الرَّدَىٰ وَلَنْ نَكُونَ لِلْعِدَىٰ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Nastaqī mina al-radá wa-lan nakūna lil-ʿidá</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We will drink of death, and we will not be to the enemies</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">كَالْعَبِيدْ كَالْعَبِيدْ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Ka-al-ʿabīd ka-al-ʿabīd</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>like slaves, like slaves.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">لَا نُرِيدْ لَا نُرِيدْ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Lā nurīd lā nurīd</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We do not want, we do not want</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">ذُلَّنَا الْمُؤَبَّدَا وَعَيْشَنَا الْمُنَكَّدَا</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Dhullanā al-muʾabbadā wa-ʿayshanā al-munakkadā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>our everlasting humiliation and our embittered life;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">لَا نُرِيدْ بَلْ نُعِيدْ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Lā nurīd bal nuʿīd</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we do not want it, but we will bring back</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">مَجْدَنَا التَّلِيدْ مَجْدَنَا التَّلِيدْ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Majdanā al-talīd majdanā al-talīd</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>our ancient glory, our ancient glory.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">مَوْطِنِي مَوْطِنِي</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Mawtinī mawtinī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My homeland, my homeland.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">مَوْطِنِي مَوْطِنِي</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Mawtinī mawtinī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My homeland, my homeland,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">اَلْحُسَامُ وَ الْيَرَاعُ لَا الْكَلَامُ وَالنِّزَاعُ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Al-husāmu wa-al-yarāʿu lā al-kalāmu wa-al-nizāʿu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the sword and the pen, not talk and quarrelling,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">رَمْزُنَا رَمْزُنَا</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Ramzunā ramzunā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>are our emblem, our emblem.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">مَجْدُنَا وَ عَهْدُنَا وَوَاجِبٌ مِنَ الْوَفَاء</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Majdunā wa-ʿahdunā wa-wājibun mina al-wafāʾ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our glory and our covenant, and a duty of faithfulness,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">يَهُزُّنَا يَهُزُّنَا</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Yahuzzunā yahuzzunā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>stir us, stir us.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">عِزُّنَا عِزُّنَا</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">ʿIzzunā ʿizzunā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our pride, our pride</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">غَايَةٌ تُشَرِّفُ وَرَايَةٌ تُرَفْرِفُ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Ghāyatun tusharrifu wa-rāyatun turafrifu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>is an aim that brings honour and a banner that flutters.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">يَا هَنَاكْ فِي عُلَاكْ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Yā hanāk fī ʿulāk</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O how happy you are in your height,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">قَاهِرًا عِدَاكْ قَاهِرًا عِدَاكْ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Qāhiran ʿidāk qāhiran ʿidāk</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>overpowering your enemies, overpowering your enemies.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">مَوْطِنِي مَوطِنِي</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Mawtinī mawtinī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My homeland, my homeland.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Ibrahim Tuqan (c. 1934); the Arabic text as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mawtini" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (ALA-LC).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (7)</summary><ul><li>Romanization: ALA-LC without the dots under letters (h, s, t, d, z for the emphatic letters), with ʿ for ʿayn and ʾ for hamza; the pausal endings (rubāk, yabīd) are written as the vocalised text has them.</li><li>line 1.5: 'hawāk' is 'your air, your breeze'; the same word also means 'love of you', and both senses may be meant.</li><li>line 1.9: al-Simāk is the Arabic name of bright stars (Arcturus and Spica); 'reaching al-Simāk' means reaching the heights of the sky.</li><li>line 2.2: 'hammuhu an yastaqilla' can be read 'their care is to be independent' (the youth themselves) or 'that you be independent'; the subject of yastaqilla is the youth's aim, the homeland's freedom.</li><li>line 2.4: 'nastaqī mina al-radá', literally 'we draw water from death', i.e. we will drink death rather than be enslaved.</li><li>line 3.2: 'al-yarāʿ' is the reed pen.</li><li>line 3.8: 'yā hanāk' is an exclamation of wished happiness, 'how blessed you are'.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
